// Cross-iteration heap-UAF: a `*T` alias is read at the TOP of a loop iteration,
// but the owner is reassigned LATER in the same iteration (freeing the pointee).
// On iteration 2+ the alias reads freed memory.  The loop fixpoint folded only
// `moved` into its re-walk entry state, so the in-loop reassign poison did not
// re-enter at the top of the next iteration and the cross-iteration dangling use
// slipped through.  Now the fixpoint also folds poison / holds_dying_borrow, so it
// is rejected.  (Re-pointing the alias before use clears the poison - see the
// positive case in the suite.)
data Box { int v; }
int f() {
  mut own *Box owner = alloc Box { v = 100 };
  *Box alias = owner;
  mut int acc = 0;
  for (int i in 0..3) {
    if (alias != null) { acc = acc + alias!.v; }   // iter 2+: alias dangles
    owner = alloc Box { v = i };                    // frees the pointee alias holds
  }
  return acc;
}
unit main() { log(f()); }
